WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.859
King Arthur: The Legend of the Sword's disastrous
opening in May 2017 vaulted it to the top

00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:11.160
of the list of biggest box office bombs of
the year, earning a measly 14.7 million dollars

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.880
in the U.S., against a $175 million budget
— plus marketing costs.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.040
"So what happens now.  You know what happens now.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.960
You're quickly becoming a legend."

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.440
Yeah: A legendary flop.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.630
But director Guy Ritchie knows how to make
big, loud, awesome action movies.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.470
Charlie Hunnam is a talented actor on-the-rise
with all the qualities you could want in a

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:35.470
leading man.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:36.980
Jude Law makes a great villain.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.060
So, by Merlin's beard, how did The Legend
of the Sword go so wrong?

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:42.900
Out of touch

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.480
The Legend of the Sword is the latest in a
string of studios trying — and failing — to

00:00:47.489 --> 00:00:52.239
put a fresh spin on old stories and properties,
joining Ben Hur, Chips, and The Legend of

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:54.039
Tarzan in the flophouse.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.860
Recycling familiar tales just isn't as profitable
as it once was, unless you're Marvel, DC,

00:00:58.860 --> 00:00:59.360
or Disney.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.540
But it wasn't that long ago that Arthurian
legend was a cinematic goldmine.

00:01:03.559 --> 00:01:08.550
Just ask the makers of 1981's Excalibur, which
grossed $35 million against an estimated

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:09.720
$11 million budget.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.280
Alas, the times have changed.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:16.920
Hollywood's last attempt at bringing the legend
to the big screen, 2004's star-packed King

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:22.000
Arthur, was also a widely panned flop, suggesting
modern audiences just no longer have an appetite

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:22.660
for Arthur ...

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.000
"Behold!

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.200
The man who pulled sword from stone!"

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:31.080
And if you actually were pumped for another
Knights of the Round Table flick, the multiple

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.890
delays pushing back the release date back
by a whopping ten months might have killed

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:35.520
your buzz.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:37.720
TV killed the cinema star

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.320
When it comes to stabbing and shouting, we're
spoiled for choice.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.450
Do we really need yet another big screen King
Arthur movie when we've got Game of Thrones

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.070
and Vikings on demand?

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:51.140
Television is the new home of the adventure
epic, with hundreds and hundreds of hours

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.200
available of the exact same kind of gritty
and bloody entertainment The Legend of the

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:55.960
Sword is offering up.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.040
Fans of swords and sorcery stories won't head
out to the theater for this kind of thing

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.010
unless they really feel like they can count
on solid results.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:07.570
And the years of recasting rumors, with Idris
Elba and Colin Farrell, among many others,

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:11.940
attached to and then dropped from the project,
did not bode well for the movie's final cut.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:13.680
Thin gruel

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.300
The almost entirely male supporting cast in
The Legend of the Sword aren't really fleshed

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:23.069
out characters like the ones fans love in
HBO's epics: they're more like plot devices.

00:02:23.069 --> 00:02:25.520
King Arthur's cast does their best with what
they've been given.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.871
But their thinly-written characters have no
real motivation outside of "find Arthur, get

00:02:29.871 --> 00:02:32.819
him to wield Excalibur, and make him tell
us what to do."

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.569
"Now, when you say, 'We're going to take the
castle,' what does that mean?"

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.200
"I thought you said you weren't going to push
me.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.240
C'mon lads, chop chop."

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.510
Then there's the mysterious Mage, the only
prominent female character, who only ever

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:51.939
uses her immense power from a safe distance
— secretly — and commands animals to do

00:02:51.939 --> 00:02:52.939
her dirty work.

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:57.080
But when the combat gets close, she's quickly
overpowered, which just doesn't track.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.460
Why is she holding back?

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.840
All in all, if your characters are a flop,
your movie probably will be, too.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:04.340
Epic fail

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:09.420
To recap: it's kind of no wonder this loud,
violent, romance-free movie with poorly written

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.689
characters bombed on Mother's Day weekend.

00:03:11.689 --> 00:03:15.499
It was beaten by the female-centric mother
and daughter comedy Snatched, as well as the

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.440
box office invincibility of Marvel and its
Guardians of the Galaxy Volume 2.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.560
Warner Brothers clearly overestimated moviegoers'
hunger for this kind of entertainment, overlooking

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:29.299
a major side effect of today's binge-watching
culture: when it doesn't leave you satisfied,

00:03:29.299 --> 00:03:33.959
it leaves you stuffed, and paying twenty bucks
at the box office for another bloated epic

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:35.319
doesn't spell relief.

00:03:35.319 --> 00:03:36.319
Thanks for watching!

00:03:36.319 --> 00:03:38.969
